Hey! Yes — getters and setters are actually pretty simple once you see them once.

## The problem they solve

You have a class with a private variable:

```java
class Student {
    private String name;   // private = nobody outside can touch it
}
```

But `private` means **no one can read or change it** from outside. That's too strict.

So you add **methods** to safely read/change it:

- **Getter** = a method to **get** (read) the value
- **Setter** = a method to **set** (change) the value

---

## The pattern

```java
class Student {
    private String name;

    // GETTER — reads the value
    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    // SETTER — changes the value
    public void setName(String newName) {
        name = newName;
    }
}
```

That's the whole pattern. **One to read, one to write.**

---

## Using them

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student();

        s.setName("Sam");           // SET the name
        System.out.println(s.getName());   // GET the name
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
Sam
```

---

## Why not just make the variable public?

Like this:

```java
class Student {
    public String name;   // anyone can change it to anything
}
```

```java
s.name = "";                    // allowed — empty name 😬
s.name = "SomeGiantStringHere"; // allowed — no control
s.name = null;                  // allowed — crash later
```

No control. Anyone can set it to anything, including garbage.

With getters/setters, you get **control**:

```java
class Student {
    private String name;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String newName) {
        if (newName == null || newName.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid name!");
        } else {
            name = newName;
        }
    }
}
```

Now if someone tries `s.setName("")`:

```
Invalid name!
```

The name stays whatever it was. **You protected the variable.**

---

## Naming rules

| Variable | Getter | Setter |
|---|---|---|
| `name` | `getName()` | `setName(...)` |
| `age` | `getAge()` | `setAge(...)` |
| `isHappy` | `isHappy()` (for booleans) | `setHappy(...)` |

- Getter: `get` + variable name (capital first letter)
- Setter: `set` + variable name (capital first letter)

---

## Full runnable example

```java
class Student {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String newName) {
        if (newName == null || newName.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println("Invalid name");
            return;
        }
        name = newName;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }

    public void setAge(int newAge) {
        if (newAge < 0 || newAge > 120) {
            System.out.println("Invalid age");
            return;
        }
        age = newAge;
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student();

        s.setName("Sam");
        s.setAge(25);

        System.out.println(s.getName() + " is " + s.getAge());

        s.setAge(-5);      // rejected
        s.setName("");     // rejected

        System.out.println(s.getName() + " is " + s.getAge());   // unchanged
    }
}
```

**Output:**
```
Sam is 25
Invalid age
Invalid name
Sam is 25
```

---

## The rule of thumb

| You want to... | Use |
|---|---|
| Read the value | Getter |
| Change the value | Setter |
| Protect the value | Make the variable `private` + getter/setter |

---

## TL;DR

- **Getter** = `getX()` → returns the value
- **Setter** = `setX(value)` → changes the value
- They let you read/change a `private` variable safely
- You can add **checks** inside setters (reject bad values)
- Naming: `get` / `set` + variable name with capital first letter

