# 👤 PART 2 — `this`

Look carefully:

```java
public void setAge(int age) {
    this.age = age;
}
```

There are **two `age`s**.

```java
this.age
     ↑
object's age
```

and

```java
age
↑
method's parameter
```

Imagine Ibrahim has a box:

```text
Ibrahim's object

┌─────────────┐
│ age = ?     │
└─────────────┘
```

Someone gives Ibrahim a new age:

```java
setAge(20)
```

The `20` comes into:

```java
age
```

Then:

```java
this.age = age;
```

means:

> **"Take the age given to me and put it into MY object's age."**

So:

```text
this.age       =       age
   ↓                     ↓
object's age       given value
```

Therefore:

```java
this.age = age;
```

means:

```text
MY age = the age you gave me
```

❤️ **That is the most important meaning of `this` for you right now.**

---

# 🧠 One tiny rule

Whenever you see:

```java
this.x
```

think:

> **"My object's x."**

For example:

```java
this.name
```

means:

> **"My object's name."**

And:

```java
this.age
```

means:

> **"My object's age."**

